WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.600
If you open an iPhone... this is what you’d see.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.009
All of these parts were put together by a
company called Foxconn.

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:11.700
And many of the parts themselves are manufactured
by Foxconn.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.059
Almost any piece of electronics has at least
some components from the company.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:21.940
Which is owned by this guy: billionaire Terry
Gou.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:27.250
He found success by making massive deals with
the world’s biggest tech companies and being

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.109
able to follow through.

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:33.490
He's always put client delivery number one,
even if at times that means taking a loss

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.940
or taking big risks on buying equipment or
setting up facilities, because he's confident

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.880
that once he lands that order, they're going
to be stuck with him.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.140
And it's usually the case.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:49.940
He’s even expanding operations into the
U.S. by building a $14.5 billion factory in Wisconsin.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:56.250
This is how one man turned a small operation
in a shed, into the biggest electronics operation

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.560
on the planet.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:06.520
Foxconn is one of the largest employers in
the world, with over 1 million workers during

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.540
peak iPhone season.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:13.020
And that’s a commodity politicians like
to offer during election time: JOBS.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.320
So when it came time for President Donald
Trump and various U.S. states to decide where

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.390
they wanted the promised factory, Gou had
them playing against each other for the best offer.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.520
And Wisconsin took the bait, and offered a
lot.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.159
They offered massive, massive incentives to
get Foxconn there.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:35.909
Really one of the largest incentive packages
in US history.

00:01:35.909 --> 00:01:41.940
Critics say the factory will most likely not
create as many jobs as promised, and people

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.140
are calling it a “white elephant” but
Gou doesn’t care.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:53.310
Wisconsin is paying him $4.5 billion dollars
in taxpayers’ money or $1800 per taxpayer.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.591
“We got a Green Bay Packers jersey with
your name on it!”

00:01:56.591 --> 00:02:02.200
At the end of the day, Terry Gou is a big
winner, and it's yet to been seen whether

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.530
Wisconsin will be a winner.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.940
Other countries have sought similar deals:
including Brazil, Indonesia and even various

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.840
Chinese provinces.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:15.920
And that’s because Gou has a reputation
for being able to pull off massive operations,

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:19.940
offering companies a one-stop shop for electronics.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:23.960
There's no other company in the world that
can do what Terry Gou does, even two decades later.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.440
Foxconn is still the master of assembly, and
it's because Terry Gou is always looking to

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.620
try and look ahead, and trying to keep customers
happy.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:40.980
Early on, Gou saw opportunity by streamlining
processes via automation and cutting out middlemen.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.760
In the 1970s Taiwan had a booming export economy.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.180
But most electronics from that time were made
in Japan, where the factories were.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.460
And then traded in Taiwan, who had the connections
into the western world.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:03:00.500
A 23 year old Gou worked as a shipping clerk
and got a first hand view of the potential

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.500
to make money.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.230
And he realized, “Wow, there's money in
this.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:06.460
Maybe I should go out on my own and do some
of this.

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:11.819
Why be the person in the middle when I can
be the one doing the actual, original production?"

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:14.670
He decided to go into business for himself.

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:21.160
Using a $7,500 loan from his mother, he rented
a shed in a gritty Taipei suburb called Tucheng,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.220
bought a couple of plastic molding machines
and started making channel-changing knobs

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.510
for black-and-white televisions.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:31.620
After that he had a series of big breaks,
starting with making components for the Atari

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.880
and later Dell.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:38.629
At that time, Dell and other PC companies
would buy parts from their suppliers and assemble

00:03:38.629 --> 00:03:40.489
them in their own factories.

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:45.550
But Gou created a production line integrating
most of these parts and processes, from the

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:50.420
raw steel for PC casings and electrical connectors
to the final assembly.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:56.220
Gou was also among the first wave of Taiwanese
to set up factories in mainland China.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:01.099
He saw the writing on the wall in the 70s,
that this was a place to do business, to build

00:04:01.099 --> 00:04:03.099
things, to manufacture things.

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:08.090
Not only at a low labor rate, but there was
just going to be plentiful staff.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:13.519
All these years later, 40 years later, it's
almost the most perfect government-corporate

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.300
partnership in history.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.070
Eventually Gou landed a deal with Apple - and
the rest is history.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:25.939
Over 50% of their estimated $165 billion revenue
now comes from Apple.

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:28.949
But it wasn’t always going smoothly.

00:04:28.949 --> 00:04:34.210
Foxconn hit international headlines in 2010
after a series of worker suicides at their

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.020
main factory in Shenzhen.

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:40.120
I give my greatest apology.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:45.349
And to put it in context, the suicide rate
in China is around 20 per 100,000.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:49.629
Terry Gou had about a million people working
for him at the top of the annual cycle.

00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:51.969
So that would imply 200 suicides a year.

00:04:51.969 --> 00:04:57.539
But they had 14, well below average, but certainly
higher than anyone expected.

00:04:57.539 --> 00:05:02.479
And because this was a company supplying to
Apple, it became famous very, very quickly

00:05:02.479 --> 00:05:06.409
as the Apple supplier and a suicide problem.

00:05:06.409 --> 00:05:12.370
After the 2010 scandals, he raised wages,
set up healthcare clinics, and even set up

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:15.430
hotlines to give advice to help workers.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:20.990
Foxconn weathered the crisis, but it was already
facing a bigger long-term issue.

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:27.189
For the past decade, revenue for electronics
hardware manufacturing has decreased and software

00:05:27.189 --> 00:05:28.800
is on the up.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.909
Almost all of the world’s biggest tech companies
are now non-hardware.

00:05:31.909 --> 00:05:38.789
Gou, who is a manufacturer at heart, hasn’t
been able to diversify products.

00:05:38.789 --> 00:05:43.219
Foxconn has been trying and trying and trying
for years to get into things like cloud services,

00:05:43.219 --> 00:05:46.289
software, but they're just not very good at it.

00:05:46.289 --> 00:05:48.099
Terry Gou doesn't have that creative thinking.

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:52.889
He doesn't look at a product and say, "How
I can I add value and charge more for it?"

00:05:52.889 --> 00:05:55.629
And that's the biggest challenge facing Foxconn
into the future.

